## Detecting changes made outside Terraform

The API never returns a context environment variable's value, so the provider
compares timestamps instead. `updated_at` records what CircleCI reported the last
time Terraform wrote the variable; `remote_updated_at` records what it reports now.
When the second is later than the first, something else wrote the variable, and the
next apply re-asserts the configured value.

Note that CircleCI bumps its timestamp on every write, including one that stores an
identical value — so any external write, even a harmless one, produces one
corrective apply.

## Contexts holding more than 100 variables

Reading this resource means listing the context: CircleCI has no route that reads
one context environment variable by name. That list stops at 100 variables and
cannot be paged past — the `next_page_token` the API advertises is ignored on every
request, whatever parameter name it is sent under.

A variable that appears in the 100 the API discloses refreshes normally. One that
does not is *ambiguous*, not absent, so refresh fails with an error naming it rather
than dropping it from state — dropping it would make the next apply recreate the
variable and overwrite whatever value is really stored on it. The API sorts by name,
so which variables fall inside the 100 depends on their names.

CircleCI caps a context at 100 environment variables, but does not apply the cap
atomically: concurrent writes can push a context past it. Split the variables across
more than one context to recover.

## Import

Import is supported using `context_id/env_var_name`:

```shell
terraform import circleci_context_environment_variable.example "<context_id>/<env_var_name>"
```

`name` and `context_id` are the only attributes import can populate. `value` cannot come along: the API never returns a context environment variable's value on any route, so it stays unset after import, and there is no way to fill it in from a read. Set it in your configuration — as `value`, or as `value_wo` plus `value_wo_version` to avoid ever writing the secret to state — before running `terraform apply` for this resource.

> **Warning:** The first `terraform plan` after import will show a diff from `null` to whatever `value` (or `value_wo_version`) your configuration sets, because Terraform has no record of the current secret to compare against. This is expected. The first `terraform apply` after import calls the same `PUT` upsert route as every other write, so it rewrites the value **in place** rather than destroying and recreating the resource — `created_at` and the environment variable's position in the context are unaffected. Because the route is an upsert either way, apply writes back whatever your configuration says, so a configuration whose `value` differs from the actual secret silently overwrites it with the wrong one; there is no read to catch the mismatch. Prefer sourcing the value from wherever the real secret already lives (e.g. a secret manager, through `value_wo`) rather than retyping it by hand.
